::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
**Ordinary least squares** of **Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100)**, 2024: 2 specifications side by side. Model (2), the last one, reads as follows.

- **Urbanization rate (% of population)**: a one-unit higher value goes with Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100) that is 0.0869 higher (statistically significant at the 1% level; 95% interval 0.0715 to 0.102), the other regressors held constant, within the same department.
- **Population (census)**: a one-unit higher value goes with Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100) that is 7.05e-06 higher (statistically significant at the 1% level; 95% interval 2.7e-06 to 1.14e-05), the other regressors held constant, within the same department.

Fixed effects for *Department* absorb every difference between their groups, so the coefficients compare observations of the same group only.
The model has 343 observations and R² = 0.449 (adjusted 0.432): the regressors and the fixed effects account for 44.9% of the variation in Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100).
Standard errors are heteroskedasticity-robust (HC1).

Across the models, the coefficient on **Urbanization rate (% of population)** is (1) 0.094, (2) 0.0869. It is stable: a coefficient that moves when regressors or fixed effects are added was partly picking up what they measure; one that stays put does not depend on them.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
:::

